# Why Roc fits Rocci

Rocci needs two things that are often pulled in opposite directions: a small,
predictable language for turning data into interfaces, and practical access to
HTTP, files, databases, windows, and operating-system services. Roc is a good
fit because it makes that boundary part of the program's architecture.

Most Rocci code can remain a pure transformation from typed values to `Html`.
The selected Roc platform and its native host provide the effects. That division
improves reasoning, testing, caching, and portability without pretending that a
real application has no effects.

> This is an architectural fit, not a maturity claim. Roc and Rocci are both
> pre-1.0 projects. The current Roc compiler is a Zig rewrite, and some older
> official documentation describes the previous Rust compiler and language
> design. The distinctions on this page are called out explicitly.

## The short version

| Rocci need | Roc property | Practical result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Deterministic component rendering | Pure functions are the default | A view can be tested as `model -> Html` without starting a server |
| Visible I/O boundaries | Effectful functions are identified with `!` and checked separately | File, network, database, and window work stays easy to find |
| Different native environments | Every app selects one platform | The same Roc-facing application model can sit on a CLI, web server, or desktop host |
| Restricted application capabilities | All Roc I/O is supplied by the platform | A purpose-built host can expose only the operations that its application needs |
| Fast feedback and native delivery | Roc defines interactive interpretation and native compiler backends over shared checked representations | A development tool can evaluate code quickly while production artifacts remain compiled |
| Reliable UI state | Structural types, tag unions, `Result`, and no null value | Missing and failure states must be modeled instead of leaking into templates |

## A pure center with an effectful edge

The natural shape of a Rocci application is a functional core surrounded by a
small effectful shell:

```text
request / event
      │
      ▼
platform operation! ──► decode data ──► update model ──► render Html
      ▲                                                        │
      └──────────── host sends response or DOM patch ◄─────────┘
```

The middle of that flow can be ordinary pure Roc. Given the same model and
props, a component produces the same `Html`. Reading a request, querying a
database, writing a file, or sending a response belongs at the edge.

Roc makes the distinction visible in names. An effectful function conventionally
ends in `!`; a pure function does not. More importantly, the compiler's effect
checking follows calls through the program: a pure function cannot invoke an
effectful one. The current compiler design also makes a useful finer distinction:
holding an effectful function value is not itself an effect; calling it is.

The established tutorial writes a pure function type with `->` and an effectful
one with `=>`. Because Roc's compiler and effect syntax are still evolving,
Rocci should treat the compiler revision and selected platform API as the source
of truth instead of copying an unpinned example from older documentation.

That maps directly onto Rocci code:

```roc
# Pure: typed page data and article Html become a complete document.
siteShell = |page, article|
    Html.fragment([header(page), navigation(page), article])

# Effectful: the outer build step reads and writes through its platform.
write_page! = |staging, item| {
    article = Path.utf8(item.article_path).read_utf8!()?
    html = siteShell(item.page, article)
    Path.utf8("${staging}/${item.output_path}").write_utf8!(html)?
}
```

The example is simplified, but the split is real in Rocs today:
`RocsTheme.siteShell` is a pure Rocci-generated function, while
`RocsBuild.run!` performs environment, file-read, and file-write operations.

### Why this matters for UI code

- **Tests stay small.** Pure components can be given records and compared with
  expected HTML without arranging a filesystem, port, clock, or window.
- **Failures have a location.** Effects are concentrated in handlers and build
  entrypoints instead of being reachable from any helper.
- **Rendering is replayable.** A fixture, server request, and static build can
  feed the same view function and compare the same result.
- **Caching becomes defensible.** Pure work can be cached by its inputs. An
  effectful function cannot silently invalidate that assumption.
- **Concurrency has fewer surprises.** Pure render work shares no hidden mutable
  state. Scheduling and cancellation remain host concerns at the edge.

Purity does not guarantee that generated HTML is accessible, secure, or fast.
It does make the renderer's inputs explicit, which gives Rocci a much better
place to validate and test those properties.

## Platforms are the capability boundary

A Roc application does not choose arbitrary I/O libraries from a global runtime.
It is combined with exactly one **platform**. The platform has two parts: a
public Roc API and a native host, commonly written in a lower-level language.
The host owns the process entrypoint, decides when to call Roc functions, handles
memory integration, and implements every I/O primitive available to the app.

Roc's standard library deliberately contains no I/O. Roc application code can
only ask for an operation that its selected platform exposes. If a minimal
documentation platform offers file reads, file writes, environment variables,
and logging—but no sockets or process spawning—ordinary Roc packages used by
that app cannot acquire those omitted operations through another I/O library.

That is unusually well matched to Rocci's possible hosts:

| Host | Useful platform surface |
| --- | --- |
| Static Rocs build | Read staged articles, render the Rocci theme, write output |
| Local web application | Receive HTTP input, query application storage, return documents or SSE patches |
| Desktop application | Web server operations plus explicitly selected dialogs, notifications, and filesystem access |
| Component preview | Load fixtures and render `Html`, ideally with no production capabilities |

This creates a concrete answer to “what can this application do?”: inspect its
platform API and the host that implements it. It also prevents a transitive Roc
package from casually importing an unrelated filesystem or network API.

The boundary should not be oversold as an operating-system sandbox. The native
host owns the process and may itself have ambient authority. A safe Rocci host
still needs careful API design, input validation, path policy, secrets handling,
and, where required, OS-level sandboxing. Roc's contribution is to make the
application-facing capability surface narrow, typed, and auditable.

## The host stays in control

In many language runtimes the framework owns `main` and user code is called by
convention. Roc platforms make that inversion explicit. The host starts the
process and calls the Roc app's exposed entrypoints when appropriate.

The platform also chooses whether an exposed operation is synchronous or
asynchronous. Rocci can therefore keep scheduling policy in the HTTP or desktop
host rather than baking one universal event-loop abstraction into every
component.

For Rocci this means a host can own the parts that are inherently environmental:

- a Rust server can convert an HTTP request into a Roc record and send the
  returned document or fragment;
- Tao and Wry can own native windows while Roc owns application data and views;
- a static generator can own discovery, routing, and atomic output commits while
  a Rocci theme owns the visible shell;
- a constrained preview host can expose fixture rendering without exposing
  production storage or native capabilities.

Roc requires one platform per application. That is a tradeoff as well as a
benefit. Platform authors must compose capabilities into one coherent contract
instead of asking an app to bolt together several independent runtimes. Normal
Roc packages can still share pure code—and effectful abstractions whose required
operations the chosen platform supports—across those hosts.

## Interpreted feedback and compiled delivery

“Interpreted and compiled” can be misleading if it suggests two different
languages. Roc's current compiler design describes one checked pipeline whose
lower-level representation can be consumed by native code generators, an
interpreter, or a serialized `LirImage`. It also describes a fast development
object backend and an LLVM backend for optimized native output. The documented
REPL evaluates expressions and definitions interactively.

```text
Rocci source
    │ lower templates
    ▼
Roc source ──► parse + canonicalize + type/effect check ──► shared IR
                                                            │
                         ┌──────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┐
                         ▼                                  ▼              ▼
                    interpreter                      dev backend     LLVM backend
                  preview / tests                  fast native run   optimized build
```

The important property for Rocci is not merely that an interpreter exists. It
is that interpretation is designed after Roc's semantic checks and shares the
compiler's lowered representations with code generation. A future Rocci preview
loop could therefore lower a component, check it with Roc, and evaluate its pure
fixture through the interpreter; deployment could compile the same code for the
native host.

Purity also gives the compiler a principled compile-time boundary. The current
design permits checked roots to be evaluated at compile time only when they have
no runtime data or control dependency and make no effectful call. For Rocci,
that could eventually precompute static view data or validate pure fixtures
without accidentally performing a network request or reading the developer's
machine during compilation.

That would give Rocci several useful modes:

1. **Editor evaluation:** render a pure component or theme function from a
   fixture without starting the complete application host.
2. **Development runtime:** compile quickly with the development backend and run
   against the real platform for handlers and I/O.
3. **Production build:** compile and optimize the application and host contract
   for distribution.
4. **Static generation:** let Rust resolve content and routes, then invoke the
   compiled Rocci theme at the narrow build boundary.

Only the last mode is how Rocs works today. Current `rocci run`, component view,
and bundle paths invoke the Roc toolchain; Rocci does not yet embed Roc's
interpreter. Interpreter-backed preview is a design opportunity, not a shipped
feature. It would require a stable embedding API, compatible value and `Html`
representations, source-map-aware diagnostics, cache invalidation, and tests
that compare interpreted and compiled results.

## Errors are values before they become pages

Roc has no `null`, `nil`, or `undefined` value. Optional and failure states use
tag unions such as `[Some a, None]` and result values such as
`[Ok value, Err problem]`. Pattern matching and `?` make those states explicit
at the point where an application decides what to do.

That matters in a document or UI system because absence is common: a route may
not exist, front matter may be incomplete, a form field may be invalid, or a
database operation may fail. Modeling each case gives the renderer enough
information to deliberately produce a not-found page, field error, retry state,
or build diagnostic. It avoids letting an implicit null travel until template
rendering fails far from its source.

Roc also aims for sound, decidable, principal whole-program type inference.
Rocci can generate ordinary Roc without making authors repeat every intermediate
type, while still leaving the Roc compiler—not the template lowerer—as the
authority on records, tags, functions, and effects.

## Why Rocs does not put everything in Roc

Choosing Roc does not mean every byte must pass through the Roc compiler. The
Rocs architecture deliberately keeps content discovery, link validation,
navigation compilation, Markdown rendering, and atomic filesystem commits in
Rust. Those jobs already operate on Rocdown's span-preserving syntax tree and
would make Roc compilation scale with prose rather than executable behavior.

Roc is used where its properties are valuable: typed theme data, pure UI
composition, authored dynamic regions, and controlled application effects. Rust
is used where the current toolchain has the stronger catalog, filesystem, and
incremental-build machinery. HTML is the explicit artifact between them.

This is not a retreat from Roc; it is the same functional-core/effectful-shell
principle applied at toolchain scale.

## Costs and open questions

Roc's fit should be evaluated with its current constraints in view:

- **Compiler transition.** Roc is a work in progress and not yet ready for a
  0.1 release. The new Zig compiler is intentionally described as bleeding edge.
- **Documentation transition.** The main tutorial targets the previous compiler,
  while the new compiler has a separate mini-tutorial and design document.
- **Platform investment.** A narrowly capable Rocci platform is powerful, but
  its ABI, memory ownership, async behavior, error mapping, and distribution all
  need to be maintained.
- **One-platform composition.** Capabilities must be designed together. That can
  improve coherence but makes ad hoc host-library composition less convenient.
- **Interpreter integration.** The compiler architecture supports it, but Rocci
  should not promise a live interpreter workflow until the embedding surface and
  semantic parity are tested against the pinned compiler revision.

For an experimental project that wants to help shape Roc-native UI conventions,
those costs are acceptable. For a production team that requires a stable
language, long-term ABI, mature package ecosystem, or supported cross-platform
installers today, they may be decisive.

## Primary sources

This page was reviewed against current first-party material in August 2026:

- [Roc platforms and applications](https://www.roc-lang.org/platforms) explains
  the one-platform model, host ownership, I/O surface, memory integration, and
  capability implications.
- [Roc FAQ](https://www.roc-lang.org/faq) covers host control, platform
  exclusivity, cross-platform packages, null-free data modeling, and type
  inference.
- [New compiler mini-tutorial](https://github.com/roc-lang/roc/blob/main/docs/mini-tutorial-new-compiler.md)
  documents the current REPL, `!` effect convention, `Result`, `expect`, and the
  status of the Zig compiler rewrite.
- [Compiler design](https://github.com/roc-lang/roc/blob/main/design.md) specifies
  effect checking, compile-time eligibility, the shared IR pipeline, interpreter,
  development object backend, LLVM backend, and explicit reference counting.
- [Roc tutorial](https://www.roc-lang.org/tutorial) gives the fuller conceptual
  account of pure and effectful functions, but currently targets the previous
  alpha compiler and should be read with that warning.
- [Roc is friendly](https://www.roc-lang.org/friendly) describes whole-program
  type inference, diagnostics, and inline `expect` tests.
- [Roc repository](https://github.com/roc-lang/roc) is the authoritative status
  reference and explicitly identifies the language as a work in progress.

Continue with [Architecture](/concepts/architecture/) to see where these
boundaries live in the repository, or [Rendering model](/concepts/rendering-model/)
to follow `Html` from a component to the browser.
